  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/odontocharacidium_cf_aphanes_en/",
      "html": "<p>A cute dwarf tetra imported from Peru. Maximum length is around 2 cm total length.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/odontocharacidium_cf_aphanes_en/",
      "html": "<p>The water parameters at a collecting site of this fish in the Rio Shishita drainage were: pH 5.32, conductivity 21 µS/cm, temperature 24°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/odontocharacidium_cf_aphanes_en/",
      "html": "<p>Despite their small size the males can be very aggressive against each other. If one can keep a lot of them together they are peaceful (the same phenomenon can be found in many cichlid species), but in a normal sized tank one should keep only one male along with one or more females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/odontocharacidium_cf_aphanes_en/",
      "html": "<p>The sexes can be distinguished easily by coloration. Only the males become bright red. Depending on mood, mating males can also develop a deep black humeral spot.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/odontocharacidium_cf_aphanes_en/",
      "html": "<p>So far only one species of the genus Odontocharacidium is known to science, namely O. aphanes from the Rio Negro in Brazil. However, this species has a rather dull coloration. It is not known whether the Peruvian fish represents a new species or a variety of O. aphanes. Keeping these very nice fish has proved unproblematic so far.</p><p>Odontocharacidium means roughly \"Characidium with teeth\"; Characidium is another genus of ground tetra. aphanes: ancient Greek, meaning \"inconspicuous\".</p>"
    }
  ],
